triage: fix_game: The tests match the rules: a trump beats the Ace of the led suit, a player void in the led suit must trump and overtrump, and a player with neither may play any card. The game breaks all of these, which points to faulty trump handling in the file, not to a test or rule problem. The game also credits too many points to declarer_points in the trump-marriage scenario.
step C: building the game file
step D: evaluating
triage: fix_game: Several independent trick-play scenarios fail in the same way: the trick points go to the wrong side, a trump does not beat a led-suit Ace, the trick winner does not lead next, and a seat is offered a card the follow and overtrump rules forbid. The tests match the written rules, so the game file's legal-card filtering, trick-winner calculation and point crediting are wrong.
step C: building the game file
triage: revise_rules: Across five attempts the coder fixed each reported problem, yet the match kept crashing in the card-handling core. The trick-winner calculation failed twice (attempts 3 and 5) and the deck draw once, which points to legality, fallback and trick bookkeeping too intricate to build reliably on a flat step list. The repeated failures, rather than one fixable slip, suggest the design itself should be simplified.
